{
  "title": "Prototype pattern",
  "idea": "The Prototype pattern creates new objects by copying a ready-made, configured instance, so callers never repeat the setup or need the concrete class.",
  "points": [
    "Use it when setup is costly; if `new` is cheap, a constructor is simpler.",
    "A registry maps keys to prototypes; `create(key)` returns a fresh copy each time.",
    "A shallow copy shares mutable fields; deep-copy them, share immutable ones.",
    "Prefer copy constructors like `new ArrayList<>(list)` to the fragile `Object.clone()`."
  ],
